In this micro_lesson, we design the overall architecture for our Full-Stack To-Do API. A RESTful API exposes endpoints for managing to-do items, typically using HTTP methods: GET to retrieve tasks, POST to create a new task, PUT to update an existing task, and DELETE to remove a task. We will use Flask as the lightweight web backend, with an in-memory dictionary to simulate a database for simplicity. Each to-do item will have an id, title, and completed status. Below is a minimal Flask application skeleton that defines the core routes and request handling structure. This architecture separates concerns: the routing layer handles HTTP requests, while the business logic layer processes data validation and state changes.

```python
from flask import Flask, jsonify, request

app = Flask(__name__)

todos = {}

@app.route('/todos', methods=['GET'])
def get_todos():
    return jsonify(list(todos.values()))

@app.route('/todos', methods=['POST'])
def create_todo():
    data = request.get_json()
    todo_id = len(todos) + 1
    todos[todo_id] = {'id': todo_id, 'title': data.get('title'), 'completed': False}
    return jsonify(todos[todo_id]), 201

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```

Key takeaways: plan your endpoints before writing code, choose an appropriate data structure for state management, and always return consistent JSON responses with correct HTTP status codes.

